 resolve of seven-year-old  Jimmy is in order to raise money for his  mom's cancer treatment or whatever it is  a thousand times more disgusting that  it's necessary in our kind  this is optional like if we decided as a  country we're not gonna be allowed in a  way that allows this we're not gonna be  organized in a way that allows this to  happen that is really the most important  thing to me so to avoid the criticism  that I know is coming I know people will  write to me and say David are you saying  that these aren't nice things people are  doing or that we shouldn't applaud them  it's fine people in bad situations  should be doing nice things and we  should recognize that and it's okay to  do that but I read this example on  reddit which is a good one someone gets  caught in the rain and a random stranger  gives them an umbrella that's nice rain  was the problem and the umbrella was the  solution in that case in a similar but  slightly different example if a homeless  person is caught in the rain for years  because they're persistently homeless  and society simply hasn't decided that  we're going to deal with that as an  issue right now and a passerby gives  them an umbrella giving them an umbrella  is still nice but the problem in the  second case is the breakdown of our  systems or the fact that they don't  exist even to begin with to deal with  homelessness as a systemic problem  that's the difference here  friendly 80 year olds who are mowing  lawns for money someone comes along and  buys them a better lawnmower that's  great that's a nice thing to do but why  are so many people unable to retire in  our country and they have to keep  working including mowing lawns out in  the heat at age 80 that's really the  problem and the new lawnmower as nice as  it might be doesn't actually solve the  underlying problem so cover the stories  that's fine but talk about structural  issues and of course corporate media  won't why not  because corporate media has not only a  bias to sensationalism but corporate  media has a bias towards  compartmentalized acute stories that  happen at one point in time rather than  systemic issues that grow or change and  shift and are difficult to cover with a  discrete beginning middle and end this  is why if 30 people died in a terrorist  attack that gets way more coverage than  10,000 starving African children dying  because even if you fix 50%  the starving African children problem  there would still be 5,000 kids who died  that day instead of 10,000 and the story  becomes background it doesn't have a  discrete beginning middle and end in the  way that corporate news basically  requires in order to fill the format  that they've decided is the one that  they're going to carry out so the faux  inspirational stories are okay as long  as we point out what the systemic  problems are corporate media is not  going to do that so at least I'll do my  best to do it for you
 

